  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the sum of all pairwise differences of outcomes between the lotteries. In this case, the outcomes are: Lottery A has -4000€ with 80% probability and 0€ with 20%; Lottery B has -3000€ with 100% probability. The pairwise difference in outcomes is (-4000€) - (-3000€) = -1000€. Since there is only one pair of outcomes (A's worst case vs B's outcome), the sum of pairwise differences is just this single difference: -1000€. The reason prefers the lottery with the more favorable (i.e., less negative or more positive) sum of outcome differences. Thus, the reason would prefer a lottery with a higher (less negative) sum of differences. Since the difference is -1000€, the sum is negative, and the reason would prefer the lottery that results in a less negative difference — which would be Lottery B, since it avoids the -4000€ outcome entirely. However, the sum of outcome differences is the same regardless of which lottery is considered, as it is a pairwise difference. The reason is based on the sum of differences across all outcome pairs, but here there is only one pair. The key is whether the individual considered the magnitude of the difference between outcomes and whether that sum was used to justify preference. The reason is applicable because the outcome differences can be computed and compared.",
